  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday: Rain Chances and Trending Cooler\n\nA line of showers and thunderstorms has formed and is \ncontinuing to move east across southeast Minnesota and western\nWisconsin. The storms should encounter a more stable \nenvironment as they move further east so the severe storm threat\nis very, very low for early this morning. The line of showers \nwith isolated storms may grow further south and quickly move \nacross the area early this morning before dissipating or moving\noff to the east near sunrise.\n\nQuiet weather is forecast for today as the next weather systems\nthat will impact the reason begin to move in over the Pacific \nNorthwest and another one attempts to dig south across the Great\nLakes region. One more day of near average high temperatures is\nforecast for today, even with a weak frontal passage early \nSaturday morning behind the line of showers/storms. Cooler air \nwill filter in on Sunday as the base of a H500 trough digs south\ntowards the Upper Midwest and highs should be around 10 degrees\ncooler than today's highs. Quick moving showers aided by weak \nsynoptic scale support may move over portions of the forecast \narea on Sunday. Any rain that does fall should be fairly light \nwith rain totals of a quarter inch or less.\n\nMonday and Tuesday: Widespread Rain with the Potential for Flooding\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system across the Pacific Northwest \nwill eject east to northeastward over the Northern Rockies towards \nSaskatchewan and Ontario late this weekend into early next week. \nThis feature will propel a surface cold front over the Northern \nPlains towards the Great Lakes during this time frame. Strong H700 \ntheta-e advection is depicted by forecast models out ahead of this \nsystem which should result in rain showers and thunderstorm \ndevelopment on Monday into Tuesday. Favorable atmospheric profiles \nalong with high PWAT values approaching 2 inches, as shown by \nblended ensemble guidance, should lead to moderate to, at times, \nheavy rainfall. Just where and when the heaviest rainfall may fall \ncontinues to vary and will be dependent upon mesoscale factors that \nwill likely continue to change over the next few days. Mean ensemble \noutput along with AI based models are trending towards the heaviest \nrainfall falling south of the area, but there could be a corridor of \nslightly higher totals (2-3 inches) across portions of the CWA. If \nheavier rainfall occurs over the area with the few rounds of rain \nthat are possible, localized flooding concerns may occur.\n\nA secondary, more uncertain, threat may arise on Monday with a \npotential for severe thunderstorms. This threat will be highly \nconditional and is dependent upon instability being able to build in \nthroughout the day. Wind shear will not be a problem as low level \nwind shear values are upwards of 40-50 kts. Trends regarding the \nsevere storm threat for Monday will need to be closely monitored \nthroughout the weekend.\n\nRest of Next Week: Rain Chances Continue with Cooler Temperatures\n\nThe weather pattern over the region will be highly dependent on the \nevolution of an upper level trough digging south across the Pacific \nNorthwest in the middle of next week. Additional rounds of showers \nor storms will be possible in the middle of the week as long as mid \nlevel moisture is in place and the trough digs far enough south. \nFurther pushes of cooler air should continue to move over the region \nin this pattern which should keep daily temperatures on the cooler \nside for the entire work week.",
